1078 #edb/ref 17592186411988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412000 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b9037e4d-7729-575a-9011-573815defbea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412001 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412001 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412001 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412001 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412001 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412001 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412001 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412001 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186411978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412001 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bfebcc4f-6a0a-526a-a3ca-b56faab77eef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412002 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412002 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412002 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186411977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412002 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c48ac98a-7a6f-5c83-b51b-ba509e7cd46c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412003 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412003 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212514" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412003 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412003 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n-11≡\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n- $-8$\n- $5$\n- $11$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412003 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "163bd7b9-110a-543a-ae84-a607fb568a3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412003 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a false statement because $8∤(-11-(-8)).$ Notice that $-11-(-8)=-3,$ which is not divisible by $8.$\n\n- Case II gives a true statement. Indeed, $8∣(-11-5).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-11-5)÷8=-16÷8=-2\n$$\n\n- Case III gives a false statement because $8∤(-11-11).$ Notice that $-11-11=-22,$ which is not divisible by $8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$5$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412004 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412004 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76978" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412004 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412004 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $7≡19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=5$\n- $n=6$\n- $n=7$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412004 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40dd9397-7325-5b4d-9df5-fd6f72af0a3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412004 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=7-19=-12.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $5∤(-12).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=5.$\n\n- Note that $6∣(-12)$ because $(-12)÷6=-2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6.$\n\n- Note that $7∤(-12).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412005 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412005 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412005 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412005 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n-10≡\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n- $-3$\n- $-1$\n- $4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412005 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8064b1f0-dca7-55dd-9b4b-ceb82032583b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412005 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a true statement. Indeed, $7∣(-10-(-3)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-10-(-3))÷7=-7÷7=-1\n$$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $7∤(-10-(-1)).$ Notice that $-10-(-1)=-9,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $7∣(-10-4).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-10-4)÷7=-14÷7=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$-3$ and $4$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412006 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103492" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412006 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $15≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$, which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=2$\n- $n=4$\n- $n=6$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8e4ac9e6-575d-537d-b731-f3556414b8f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412006 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=15-7=8.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∣8$ because $8÷2=4,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $4∣8$ because $8÷4=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=4.$\n\n- Note that $6∤8.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412007 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412007 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212522" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412007 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412007 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $-7≡-3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=2$\n- $n=3$\n- $n=7$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412007 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd2ee89b-0850-5297-a810-7e54facce40b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412007 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=-7-(-3)=-4.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∣(-4)$ because $(-4)÷2=-2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $3∤(-4).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $7∤(-4).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412008 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186412009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412008 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4a965a5a-3116-51ac-985b-7ca74b4d914f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412008 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186412003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412009 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "98b18ba5-e488-5d43-92d3-11d090b1312a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412009 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186412007 #edb/ref 175921